<article class="env-lemma" id="0CUK">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0CUK">Lemma <span data-tag="0CUK">70.5.4</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $X = \mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits _{i \in I} X_ i$ be the limit of a directed inverse system of algebraic spaces over $S$ with affine transition morphisms (Lemma <a href="/tag/07SF" data-tag="07SF">70.4.1</a>). Let $x \in |X|$ with images $x_ i \in |X_ i|$. If each $X_ i$ is decent, then $\overline{\{ x\} } = \mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits _ i \overline{\{ x_ i\} }$ as sets and as algebraic spaces if endowed with reduced induced scheme structure. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Set $Z = \overline{\{ x\} } \subset |X|$ and $Z_ i = \overline{\{ x_ i\} } \subset |X_ i|$. Since $|X| \to |X_ i|$ is continuous we see that $Z$ maps into $Z_ i$ for each $i$. Hence we obtain an injective map $Z \to \mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits Z_ i$ because $|X| = \mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits |X_ i|$ as sets (Lemma <a href="/tag/0CUJ" data-tag="0CUJ">70.5.1</a>). Suppose that $x' \in |X|$ is not in $Z$. Then there is an open subset $U \subset |X|$ with $x' \in U$ and $x \not\in U$. Since $|X| = \mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits |X_ i|$ as topological spaces (Lemma <a href="/tag/086V" data-tag="086V">70.5.2</a>) we can write $U = \bigcup _{j \in J} f_ j^{-1}(U_ j)$ for some subset $J \subset I$ and opens $U_ j \subset |X_ j|$, see Topology, Lemma <a href="/tag/0A2P" data-tag="0A2P">5.14.2</a>. Then we see that for some $j \in J$ we have $f_ j(x') \in U_ j$ and $f_ j(x) \not\in U_ j$. In other words, we see that $f_ j(x') \not\in Z_ j$. Thus $Z = \mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits Z_ i$ as sets. 
    <p>
      Next, endow $Z$ and $Z_ i$ with their reduced induced scheme structures, see Properties of Spaces, Definition <a href="/tag/047X" data-tag="047X">66.12.5</a>. The transition morphisms $X_{i'} \to X_ i$ induce affine morphisms $Z_{i'} \to Z_ i$ and the projections $X \to X_ i$ induce compatible morphisms $Z \to Z_ i$. Hence we obtain morphisms $Z \to \mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits Z_ i \to X$ of algebraic spaces. By Lemma <a href="/tag/0CUH" data-tag="0CUH">70.4.3</a> we see that $\mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits Z_ i \to X$ is a closed immersion. By Lemma <a href="/tag/0CUI" data-tag="0CUI">70.4.4</a> the algebraic space $\mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits Z_ i$ is reduced. By the above $Z \to \mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits Z_ i$ is bijective on points. By uniqueness of the reduced induced closed subscheme structure we find that this morphism is an isomorphism of algebraic spaces. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>